(* ==========================================================================
   corpus.bnf — WHAT A CORPUS IS MADE OF.

   `grammar.bnf` says the SHAPE of a program. `catalogue.bnf` says the NAMES a
   program may write. This says the names the WRITER emits and every reader has
   to know — the third data file, and the one that was missing.

   Reference: the same shape as `catalogue.bnf`, same dialect, same lexer. Not a
   second mechanism.

   # Why this file exists

   Of the three boundaries this system has — program, plan, artefact — two were
   specified by a grammar that generates code and the third was not. The corpus
   format is the only one of the three that crosses a process, a language and a
   network, and it was specified in prose and re-implemented per language.

   Measured before this file existed, over `crates/`, `packages/` and `apps/`:
   **545 string literals for eight words** — `dense_id` 190 times, `subject` 74,
   `src_dense`/`dst_dense` 134, `by_source`/`by_target` 73, `cluster_id` 50,
   `tiles.parquet` 34 — with no declaration any of them derived from.

   And they had already drifted. «The columns the writer emits» was stated in
   three places with **two different contents**:

     crates/fossil-graph/src/manifest.rs   five names, `cluster_id` included
     packages/corpus/src/corpus.ts         four names, `cluster_id` absent
     apps/playground/src/encoding.ts       the same set spelled a third way

   Neither is wrong where it stands, and that is the point: they mean different
   things — *every column the writer emits* against *the columns this struct
   already surfaces as named members* — and nothing said so, because there was
   nowhere for a set to be named. A `role` is what tells them apart now, and each
   reader names the roles it means.

   # The `type` is the spelling the MANIFEST writes

   Not Arrow's and not Rust's. `crates/fossil-sinks/src/manifest.rs, data_type_name`
   is the mapping from an Arrow type to it, and a `properties:` entry carries
   whatever that returns — so a column declared here with a different spelling
   for the same type would put two names for one thing in one document, which is
   the defect this file exists to remove.

   That was live and cost nothing to find: `x` and `y` read `float32` here while
   the manifest wrote `float` for the same column, and it stayed invisible for as
   long as nothing read this field. A cell row's columns go into a manifest, so it
   is read now.

   `uint32` and `uint64` are fossil's own, and the format says so: GraphAr defines
   no unsigned type and its reference reader throws on `dense_id`. See the header
   of `crates/fossil-sinks/src/manifest.rs`.

   # What is data and what is not

   Same split as `catalogue.bnf`. The NAME, the TYPE and the ROLE are data and
   live here. What a reader DOES with a role — hide it from a field listing,
   surface it as a member, colour by it — is the reader's decision and stays
   where it is made.

   # What is NOT here, on purpose

   **Prefixes and file names** (`tiles.parquet`, `by_source/`, `index/`,
   `l{k}/`, `graph.graph.yml`). They are addressing, and addressing is
   `crates/fossil-graph/src/plan.rs` — one implementation already, compiled to
   wasm32 for the TypeScript side. A second declaration of them here would be a
   second source for a fact that already has one. Columns are different: nothing
   owns them today, which is why they drifted.

   **`TILE_SHIFT` and `STRIDE_BITS`.** They are in
   `crates/fossil-sinks/src/manifest.rs` and every reader derives from them
   there. Same reason.

   # Status

   `cargo xtask corpus` generates `crates/fossil-sinks/src/generated.rs` and
   `packages/corpus/src/vocabulary.generated.ts` from this file, and
   `cargo xtask corpus --check` fails when a checked-in output is stale.
   `crates/xtask/tests/corpus_generated.rs` runs that check, so `cargo test`
   catches an edit to this file that was not regenerated, and an edit to a
   generated file by hand. There is no CI step for it, for the reason
   `catalogue.bnf` has none.
   ========================================================================== *)

(* ------------------------------------------------------------------ roles *)
(* A role says what a column IS, not what anyone does with it.

   address      the row's position in the ordering. `dense_id` is the vertex's
                rank in Morton order of its position, so it is an ADDRESS and
                never an identity: re-running the layout renumbers every vertex.
                `/docs/design/identity` is the argument.
   identity     what a bookmark keys on and what survives a rebuild.
   coordinate   the plane. Declared as a pair, drawn as a pair.
   categorical  an ordinal the writer computed, for a reader to colour by.
   endpoint     one end of a relation, in the aligned type's `dense_id` space.
   tally        how many rows of the level below a summary row stands for.
   weight       a summed edge weight.
   mode         the majority value of a categorical over the rows summarised.
   purity       what fraction of those rows carry the mode.
   incident     one end of a quotient edge, in its own rung's cell space. NOT an
                `endpoint`: an endpoint indexes a vertex type's `dense_id`, and
                these index the rung's own cells, which is a different space with
                a different width at every rung. Sharing the role would have
                given the quotient an `aligns` it has no use for.

   `declared` marks a column that also appears in the manifest's `properties:`.
   Exactly one does. That asymmetry is the whole of the manifest-versus-bytes
   difference `packages/corpus/src/corpus.ts` documents from the other side: on
   the conformance corpus it is three declared properties against seven columns
   on disk, and this file is where the three-versus-seven is stated once. *)

(* ------------------------------------------------------- the vertex payload *)
(* In writer order, which is the order `fossil-df` prepends and `fossil-layout`
   replaces. A reader may project any subset; the order is what the two writers
   agree on. *)

col dense_id   = in payload ; type uint32  ; role address .
col subject    = in payload ; type string  ; role identity    ; declared .
col x          = in payload ; type float   ; role coordinate .
col y          = in payload ; type float   ; role coordinate .
col cluster_id = in payload ; type uint32  ; role categorical .

(* ----------------------------------------------------------- the adjacency *)
(* Both orientations carry the same two columns and differ only in row order —
   `by_source` is sorted by `src_dense`, `by_target` by `dst_dense`. `aligns`
   names which one a given orientation is tiled by, which is the column a tile
   of that orientation filters on. *)

col src_dense = in adjacency ; type uint32 ; role endpoint ; aligns src .
col dst_dense = in adjacency ; type uint32 ; role endpoint ; aligns dst .

(* ------------------------------------------------------------- the pyramid *)
(* A cell row is one texel: a region of the plane, summarised. One rung of the
   pyramid is a set of them, and `/docs/design/holons` is the argument for why it
   is a third artefact and not a coarse `Projection`.

   Two columns of the payload are NOT here and their absence is the design:

   - no `parent`. A cell of rung `k` covers exactly `[r·4^k, (r+1)·4^k)` of the
     `dense_id` axis, so the parent of cell `r` is `r >> 2` and the id carries
     it. A stored parent would be a second statement of a shift.
   - no `subject`. A cell is not an entity and no bookmark keys on one; the same
     argument that keeps it out of `properties:` keeps it out of here.

   `x` and `y` are deliberately the payload's own names. A reader drawing a cell
   draws it exactly as it draws a vertex, and a second spelling would make the
   camera ask which artefact it is holding. *)

col cell_id  = in cell ; type uint32  ; role address .
col x        = in cell ; type float   ; role coordinate .
col y        = in cell ; type float   ; role coordinate .
col count    = in cell ; type uint32  ; role tally .
col internal = in cell ; type uint64  ; role weight .
col mode     = in cell ; type uint32  ; role mode .
col purity   = in cell ; type float   ; role purity .

(* ------------------------------------------------------------- the quotient *)
(* The edges between one rung's cells, beside that rung's rows. A separate
   artefact because of arity and not preference: a cell row is one cell and a
   quotient edge is a pair of them, which is the same reason a corpus writes its
   vertices and its relations apart.

   An edge whose two ends share a cell is NOT an edge of that cell — it is
   absorbed into the cell's `internal`. On the planted fixture
   `crates/fossil-layout/tests/holons.rs` evaluates, 1,088 of 1,095 edges are
   absorbed and the quotient has seven, so a writer that relabels the endpoints
   and keeps the set is not wrong in an edge case. *)

col src_cell = in quotient ; type uint32 ; role incident .
col dst_cell = in quotient ; type uint32 ; role incident .
col weight   = in quotient ; type uint64 ; role weight .
